# Keep `stylua .` confined to first-party Lua — the prelude and the examples.
# Vendored neovim copies — never reformat (diverges from upstream).
crates/bemtvi-lua/src/vendor/
# Out of the pre-commit's chosen scope (the prelude and examples/).
crates/bemtvi/tests/
vendor/
# An example's sample BUFFERS are content, not code: the reader OPENS them and types
# at them, so their shape is the fixture. `rainbow/sample.lua` is deliberately thick
# with redundant parentheses (there is nothing to colour otherwise) and
# `buffer-local-options/two.lua` deliberately leaves a line at column 0 so `>>` has
# somewhere to move it. Formatting either deletes the thing being demonstrated. The
# rule is structural — a bare `.lua` in an example's root is a fixture; its `init.lua`,
# its `test/` specs and any module/plugin tree under it are code, and stay formatted.
examples/*/*.lua
!examples/*/init.lua
# Outside the hook's scope, and deliberately hand-formatted:
# the colorscheme's palette is column-aligned (`bg          = "#282c34"`), which is how
# a palette stays readable and which stylua cannot express, and the demo seed defines
# one snippet PER ROW — stylua would explode each into a five-line block while still
# not fixing the only over-width lines there (embedded `body` strings, which can't wrap).
crates/bemtvi-server/runtime/colors/
crates/bemtvi-edithost/web/demo-seed/
